{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":189172,"duration_api_ms":188057,"num_turns":21,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"06f95573-def3-4c02-9d35-c224b6fbd359","total_cost_usd":0.8969766499999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":243,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52822,"cache_read_input_tokens":717022,"output_tokens":4991,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52822,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":243,"outputTokens":4991,"cacheReadInputTokens":717022,"cacheCreationInputTokens":52822,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.8146384999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":90,"outputTokens":4988,"cacheReadInputTokens":346244,"cacheCreationInputTokens":18147,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.08233815000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In `JsonLinesSink::emit_tick` and `StderrSink::emit_tick` (tracing.rs:124-128, 135-139), both serialization errors (`serde_json::to_string`) and write errors (`writeln!`) are silently swallowed. For CI archival use — one of the three stated output modes — silently losing trace records makes it impossible to detect data loss. At minimum, consider logging or counting dropped records. For `JsonLinesSink` specifically, the `let _ = writeln!` could be changed to propagate or record the error, since file I/O failures during CI runs are not uncommon (disk full, broken pipe). Alternatively, change `TraceSink::emit_tick` to return a `Result` so callers can decide the policy.","rationale":"The acceptance criteria require 'file output mode produces valid JSON-lines that can be piped to jq or diffed across runs.' Silently swallowing write errors means a CI pipeline could produce a truncated trace file with no error signal, leading to false-passing regression diffs."},{"body":"The manual `Clone` implementation for `Engine` (engine.rs:42-51) silently drops the tracer by setting it to `None` in the clone. There is no documentation on this behavior — neither a doc comment on the `Clone` impl, nor a note on `enable_tracing`. Downstream beads that clone engines (e.g., for forking scenarios or speculative rollbacks) will silently lose their tracing configuration. Add a doc comment on the `Clone` impl explaining that tracer is not cloned because `Box<dyn TraceSink>` is not `Clone`, so callers must re-enable tracing on cloned engines.","rationale":"Multiple dependent beads (A2e harness, scenario probes) will create engine clones. Silent tracer loss would produce confusing missing-trace-data bugs that are hard to diagnose. A doc comment costs nothing and prevents wasted debugging time."},{"body":"The `PoolSnapshot::deltas_to` method (tracing.rs:227-245) uses `f64::EPSILON` (~2.2e-16) as the threshold for reporting pool changes. This is extremely sensitive — for pool values in typical simulation ranges (0.01 to 1000), even innocuous arithmetic like `x = x * 1.0` or reading/writing through an intermediate can produce rounding noise that exceeds this threshold. Consider using a relative-or-absolute threshold, e.g. `delta.abs() > 1e-12 || (delta / before_val).abs() > 1e-12`, to suppress sub-picogram noise while still catching real sub-microgram changes relevant to budget tracking.","rationale":"The acceptance criteria state 'file output mode produces valid JSON-lines that can be diffed across runs.' If the threshold is too sensitive, trace diffs across identical runs on different platforms (or compiler versions) will produce false positives from floating-point noise, undermining the regression comparison use case."}],"summary":"The structured simulation tracing implementation is clean, well-designed, and meets the acceptance criteria. All 20 tests (5 unit + 15 integration) pass. The core architecture — `Option<SimTracer>` on Engine, `TickTraceBuilder` pattern, `PoolSnapshot` before/after deltas, verbosity-gated allocation — is sound. Verbosity::Off correctly short-circuits all work (no snapshots, no allocations, no sink writes). The JSON schema is stable and round-trips correctly. Budget tracking and tracing coexist without interference. Three issues identified: (1) sink error swallowing risks silent data loss for CI file output, (2) `Engine::clone()` silently drops the tracer without documentation, (3) the `f64::EPSILON` delta threshold is too sensitive for cross-platform regression diffing."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"048e51cc-4d96-4bf2-a74a-0842c63cc4a5"}
